## 4. Storage
**Concept:** Block, object, file, archive, and hybrid connectors for different access patterns and latency requirements.

Key services:
- Amazon S3 — object storage (Standard, Intelligent-Tiering, IA, Glacier, Deep Archive).
- Amazon EBS — persistent block storage for EC2.
- EC2 Instance Store — ephemeral, fast local storage (lost on stop/terminate).
- Amazon EFS — NFS-based shared file system for Linux clients.
- Amazon FSx (Lustre / Windows) — high-performance file systems for specific workloads.
- AWS Backup & Storage Gateway — centralized backups and hybrid access.

When to use:
- S3 for static objects and data lakes.
- EBS for OS and DB disks.
- EFS for shared files between multiple EC2 instances.
- FSx for specialized throughput or Windows SMB needs.

Exam tips:
- Know S3 consistency and lifecycle transitions to Glacier.
- Snapshots vs backups: EBS snapshots are incremental and stored in S3 behind the scenes.

![When to use S3 vs EBS vs EFS vs FSx](images/storage-decision-diagram.png)

